Start the new image of the writer after a binary change

A package update replaces the binary of the writer. The daemon kept the
old image until the next login, and the sheets stayed old.

The daemon now watches its own image path and the directory of that
path. A package manager unlinks the old file and extracts a new inode. A
watch of the file alone therefore dies with the file that it watches.

The daemon runs one probe of the new image before it starts it. A new
image can be half extracted, or it can miss a library, or it can give
the code 1. A daemon that starts such an image dies with it. The probe
is the sheet mode, which reads no bus, takes no lock, needs no display
and writes no file. A probe that fails keeps the old image.

The daemon reads the path again only after another change of the file. A
file that stays broken gives one line and not a stream.

The probe does not use the saved arguments. A pass that changes the
files of the user in the middle of a swap costs more. A new image that
drops a flag starts and then stops at the parse.

execv(2) keeps the pid, and a lock of flock(2) belongs to the open file
description. The daemon lock therefore crosses the exec with no open
window. The new image takes the descriptor over after it reads st_dev
and st_ino of the descriptor and of the lock path.

An image that no directory holds again leaves the old image alive. A
different program at the image path also runs, because the path is the
contract of the operator.

The change sits on the branch work/gtk4-theme of the local tree, as the
ninth commit. The fork wyn/qtcurve holds that branch. Merge request !15 on
system/qtcurve holds that branch.

Drop this patch when upstream merges the change.

